  "query_preview": "..\nA cold front crosses the area overnight with showers. \n\nThe latest surface map shows a weak area of low pressure across\nNew York State and a cold front into Pennsylvania. A mild and\nincreasingly flow ahead of these features has brought some\nshowers to parts of the region so far today. More showers and\npossibly a tstm will continue to develop across PA/MD and move\neast into NJ/DE this evening and into the overnight. We'll\ncontinue with the higher CHANCE and low-end LIKELY pops for \nthese time periods. Rains will end by dawn but some patchy rural\nfog is possible. \n\nWhile severe weather is not expected, there will likely be some\nshort period downpours this evening with some rainfall rates\naround an inch per hour possible. The moderately fast motion of\nthe cells will preclude any significant totals however. Average\nrainfall of 0.1 to 0.3 inches possible with a few totals closer\nto 1.0 inch possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A series of cold fronts and low pressure systems\nwill bring unsettled weather to the area starting Saturday night\nand continuing through at least Tuesday. Heavy rain from time \nto time is possible.\n\nHigh pressure will be centered over the Province of Quebec Saturday \nmorning, and then the center of the high will shift towards the \nCanadian Maritimes and the Gulf of Maine by Saturday evening. \nOverall, conditions should start out mostly sunny Saturday morning, \nthen mid and high clouds will build into the region throughout the \nday. Mild and pleasant otherwise with highs in the 70s to near 80.\n\nHigh pressure moves out to sea Saturday night, and then low pressure \norganizes and develops over the Midwest. A warm front extending out \nfrom that low will lift north through the Northeast Saturday night \nthrough Sunday morning. Showers first develop after midnight \nSaturday night and continue through Sunday morning.\n\nAs low pressure draws nearer to the region, southerly flow increases \nthroughout the Northeast and low level moisture increases as well \nwith surface dew points rising well into the 60s and low 70s and \nPWATs will be in excess of 2 inches. Low pressure then passes \nthrough the southern Poconos and Lehigh Valley Sunday afternoon and \nevening, resulting in more widespread showers with isolated \nthunderstorms across most of the area during that time. Locally \nheavy rain may develop from time to time. A cold front passes \nthrough the region behind the departing low, and that front will sag \nsouth towards Delmarva by Monday morning, where it will stall out. \n\nWPC has a Marginal risk for Excessive Rainfall (1 out of 4) for the \nregion Sunday and Monday.\n\nSeveral more disturbances will pass through the region Monday and \nTuesday, and this will keep rain chances in the forecast during this \ntime. For now, PoPs will mostly be capped at 30 to 50 percent \n(chance).",
